WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.571
- A fascinating legal
issue has come up again

00:00:01.571 --> 00:00:03.300
in the Derek Chauvin trial,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.840
specifically with respect
to third degree murder.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.930
Now, we've talked about this
in the past, on this channel.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.360
Third degree murder is not as severe

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.700
as second or first degree murder,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:11.909
where you're doing
something that is clearly

00:00:11.909 --> 00:00:13.929
going to injure other people.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:16.130
So it wasn't a surprise that
prosecutors charged Chauvin

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.850
with that particular crime.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.130
But there was language
in the Minnesota statute

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.040
that made it seem like,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.870
you had to direct that sort of conduct

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:23.940
towards multiple people.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.260
You couldn't direct that kind of conduct

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.860
towards one particular person.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.630
Well, the Minnesota Court of
Appeals stepped in and said,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:29.463
no, that's not the case,

00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:31.760
you can actually direct it
towards one particular person

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.405
and still be guilty of
third degree murder.

00:00:33.405 --> 00:00:36.150
Andrew from opening arguments was right.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.530
He was leading the charge and said,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.390
the interpretation of this law that said

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.630
you couldn't direct it towards one person

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:41.463
didn't make any sense.

00:00:41.463 --> 00:00:43.480
The Minnesota Supreme
Court could still step in

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.040
and reverse the Court of Appeals,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.502
but it does appear that
Chauvin was correctly charged

00:00:47.502 --> 00:00:48.770
with third degree murder

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.620
and the jury will go forward deliberating

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.920
on that particular charge.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.957
Go listen to one of my
favorite legal podcasts,

00:00:54.957 --> 00:00:57.483
"Opening Arguments", or
I will see you in court.

